# ── Re-includes for already-tracked paths (#2873) ────────────────────────────
# `git ls-files | git check-ignore` reported 80 tracked files as ignored. Most
# were negation patterns above doing their job. Twelve were real: rules aimed at
# generated reports and local scratch were also covering curated, deliberately
# tracked files. Narrowed here with negations rather than by untracking, because
# every path below is either referenced documentation or a live CI input.
#
# Appended as a single block on purpose — two other branches are editing this
# file, so nothing above is reordered or reformatted.

# The "*AUDIT*.md" / "UI-AUDIT-*.md" rules target generated audit dumps, but they
# also matched curated docs: docs/audits/ is a maintained directory, AUDIT_EVENTS
# and INTERNAL_TOOLS_AUDIT_SUMMARY are linked from docs/README.md and
# docs/product/DATA_ROOM_INDEX.md, and the two frontend files are hand-maintained
# (frontend/scripts/promise-audit.mjs only reads, it never writes them).
!docs/audits/*.md
!docs/engineering/AUDIT_EVENTS.md
!docs/internal/INTERNAL_TOOLS_AUDIT_SUMMARY.md
!frontend/PAGE_AUDIT.md
!frontend/PROMISE_AUDIT.md

# sales_enablement/ is NOT safe to untrack: scripts/check_trust_safety_copy.py
# scans it (SCAN_ROOTS) and frontend-ci.yml runs that check on every PR touching
# these paths, so removing the kit would drop it out of the trust-and-safety copy
# gate. Re-include the directories, re-ignore their contents, then allow only
# the two files that are actually tracked. Deliberately exact filenames, not a
# *.md glob: the block below warns that local sales work may carry personal
# contact data, so a new markdown file here should stay ignored until someone
# consciously adds it.
!sales_enablement/
sales_enablement/*
!sales_enablement/fsma_204_sales_enablement_kit_v2.md
!sales_enablement/_archive/
sales_enablement/_archive/*
!sales_enablement/_archive/fsma_204_sales_enablement_kit_v1.1.md

# output/ holds generated pilot artifacts and stays ignored. The one exception is
# the README that documents that rule — untracking it would delete the very
# warning that tells readers not to treat output/pilots/ as source of truth.
!output/
output/*
!output/pilots/
output/pilots/*
!output/pilots/README.md
